Proposed experiment: Neural Network Structural Adaptation
1. Build a neural-style network
   
   Use simulated neurons, connections, activation signals, and a simple learning task.
2. Introduce your structural rules
   
   Allow the network to create and remove connections and update its state partitions.
3. Compare against established alternatives
   
   Include fixed connectivity, random pruning, activity-dependent pruning, and conventional structural plasticity rules.
4. Measure actual computational costs
   
   Record connection count, signal transmissions, computational operations, and an explicit energy-cost estimate.
5. Test whether performance survives
   
   Measure accuracy, learning speed, memory retention, robustness to damaged connections, and performance on unfamiliar inputs.
6. Remove the partition mechanism
   
   Compare the complete model against a version that only changes connections. This isolates whether partition refinement contributes anything.
